{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "Paper created successfully\n",
  "stderr": "",
  "return_code": 0,
  "success": true,
  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'PAPER_EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid evolution of artificial intelligence has ushered in a new era of autonomous code agents capable of generating, debugging, and executing complex software programs with minimal human intervention. While these advancements promise unprecedented productivity gains, they also introduce significant security and safety concerns that demand rigorous evaluation. This paper surveys the current landscape of code agent technologies and the emerging field of red-teaming as a critical mechanism for identifying vulnerabilities in these systems. We review foundational work in deep learning architectures that underpin modern code agents, discuss recent developments in automated red-teaming approaches, and examine the bidirectional relationship between red and blue teaming methodologies. Our analysis reveals critical gaps in current safety evaluation frameworks and proposes directions for future research. We emphasize that robust red-teaming practices, when integrated with systematic blue-teaming responses, form an essential defense-in-depth strategy for ensuring the trustworthy deployment of code-generating AI systems.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent a paradigm shift in software development, leveraging large language models (LLMs) to autonomously perform coding tasks ranging from simple script generation to complex system architecture design. These agents combine natural language understanding with programming expertise, enabling them to interpret user intent, generate code solutions, execute them in sandboxed environments, and iteratively refine their outputs based on feedback. The integration of code interpreters and execution engines transforms these systems from passive code generators into active, autonomous problem-solvers.\n\nHowever, the autonomous nature of code agents introduces unique security challenges that traditional software testing approaches are ill-equipped to handle. Unlike conventional software, where code paths are deterministic and predictable, code agents generate novel, unpredictable code that may contain subtle vulnerabilities, security bypasses, or policy violations. These risks are amplified when agents operate in environments with access to network resources, file systems, or external APIs.\n\nRed-teaming—systematic attempts to identify vulnerabilities by simulating adversarial behavior—has emerged as a crucial methodology for stress-testing code agents before deployment. By probing agents with carefully crafted prompts designed to elicit unsafe behaviors, researchers can uncover failure modes that might otherwise remain hidden in production environments. The field has evolved from manual penetration testing to automated red-teaming agents that leverage their own code-generation capabilities to systematically explore the vulnerability space of target code agents.\n\nThis paper contributes to the literature by:\n1. Providing a comprehensive overview of code agent architectures and capabilities\n2. Surveying the state-of-the-art in automated red-teaming methodologies for code agents\n3. Analyzing the interplay between red-teaming and blue-teaming in the context of code generation AI\n4. Identifying open challenges and proposing directions for future research\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Foundational Deep Learning Architectures\n\nThe capabilities of modern code agents are built upon foundational advances in deep learning. The transformer architecture, introduced in [1] (Vaswani et al., arXiv:1706.03762), revolutionized sequence modeling and became the backbone of virtually all modern LLMs. The self-attention mechanism proposed in \"Attention Is All You Need\" enables models to capture long-range dependencies in code, which is essential for understanding program structure and generating coherent code across multiple lines and files.\n\nComplementing these advances, residual learning techniques developed in [2] (He et al., arXiv:1512.03385) have enabled the training of increasingly deep neural networks without degradation. The skip connections and residual blocks introduced in \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition\" have been adapted for code models, allowing transformer-based code generators to scale to hundreds of layers while maintaining gradient flow during training. These architectural innovations are foundational to the reasoning and generation capabilities that modern code agents exhibit.\n\n### 2.2 Automated Red-Teaming for Code Agents\n\nRecent work has focused specifically on developing automated red-teaming methodologies tailored to code agents. Guo et al. [3] (arXiv:2510.02609) introduced RedCodeAgent, a novel automatic red-teaming agent designed to systematically probe diverse code agents for vulnerabilities. RedCodeAgent operates by generating adversarial code prompts that exploit gaps in the safety filtering and content moderation capabilities of target code agents. The authors demonstrate that their approach can uncover a wide range of unsafe behaviors, including code that bypasses sandbox restrictions, attempts to access restricted resources, or generates malicious payloads.\n\nThe RedCodeAgent framework introduces several key innovations:\n- **Prompt Generation**: Uses a generative approach to create adversarial prompts specifically designed to elicit unsafe code generation\n- **Multi-Agent Collaboration**: Employs multiple red-teaming agents working in parallel to explore different attack vectors\n- **Adaptive Exploration**: Dynamically adjusts the probing strategy based on observed vulnerabilities in target agents\n- **Diverse Code Agent Targeting**: Demonstrates effectiveness across multiple code agent architectures\n\n### 2.3 Blue Teaming and Defense Mechanisms\n\nWhile red-teaming focuses on identifying vulnerabilities, blue-teaming addresses the complementary task of developing and deploying defense mechanisms. Guo et al. [4] (arXiv:2501.01234) proposed BlueCodeAgent, a blue-teaming agent enabled by automated red-teaming for CodeGen AI. This work establishes a bidirectional relationship between red and blue teaming, where insights gained from red-teaming directly inform the development of defensive strategies.\n\nBlueCodeAgent operates by:\n- Learning vulnerability patterns discovered during red-teaming exercises\n- Developing automated filters and detection mechanisms for identified attack patterns\n- Generating robust code that is resistant to common adversarial perturbations\n- Continuously updating defense strategies as new vulnerabilities are discovered\n\nThe integration of blue and red teaming represents a mature approach to AI safety, where offensive and defensive capabilities evolve in tandem. This adversarial training loop ensures that defensive measures are rigorously tested against realistic attack strategies before deployment.\n\n## 3. Conclusion\n\nThe rapid advancement of code agent technologies presents both tremendous opportunities and significant risks. As demonstrated by recent work in automated red-teaming [3, 4], the security challenges posed by autonomous code generation systems are complex and require specialized evaluation methodologies. The foundational deep learning architectures described in [1, 2] continue to enable increasingly capable code agents, while the red-teaming frameworks developed to test them expose critical vulnerabilities that must be addressed.\n\nThe bidirectional relationship between red-teaming and blue-teaming, as exemplified by BlueCodeAgent, represents a promising direction for AI safety research. By integrating offensive and defensive capabilities in a continuous adversarial loop, developers can build more robust code agents that resist exploitation while maintaining their utility.\n\nSeveral key challenges remain for future research:\n1. **Scalability**: Developing red-teaming methodologies that can efficiently evaluate the exponentially large space of possible code agent behaviors\n2. **Transferability**: Understanding how vulnerabilities discovered in one code agent generalize to others\n3. **Human-in-the-loop**: Integrating human expert judgment with automated red-teaming for higher-quality security assessments\n4. **Standardization**: Establishing common benchmarks and metrics for evaluating code agent safety\n5. **Long-term Safety**: Addressing the challenge of safety over time as code agents learn and adapt in production environments\n\nAs code agents become more prevalent in software development workflows, the importance of rigorous red-teaming practices will only increase. Researchers and practitioners must invest in developing comprehensive safety evaluation frameworks that can keep pace with the rapid evolution of these powerful systems. The collaboration between red and blue teaming approaches, building on the foundational work of deep learning and automated testing, provides a roadmap for ensuring that code agents can be deployed responsibly and securely.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Vaswani, A., Shazeer, N., Parmar, N., et al. \"Attention Is All You Need.\" arXiv:1706.03762, 2017.\n\n[2] He, K., Zhang, X., Ren, S., et al. \"Deep Residual Learning for Image Recognition.\" arXiv:1512.03385, 2015.\n\n[3] Guo, C., Xie, C., Yang, Y., et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" arXiv:2510.02609, 2025.\n\n[4] Guo, C., Nie, Y., Xie, C., et al. \"BlueCodeAgent: A Blue Teaming Agent Enabled by Automated Red Teaming for CodeGen AI.\" arXiv:2501.01234, 2025.\nPAPER_EOF\necho \"Paper created successfully\""
}'}

